      <p class="kicker">Quick self-check</p>
      <h2>Three things to look at before you call</h2>
      <p><strong>1. The switchboard.</strong> Cylinders have their own fuse or breaker, usually labelled "HWC". If it's tripped, flick it back once. Trips again? Stop — that's an element or wiring fault. Call.</p>
      <p><strong>2. Water around the cylinder.</strong> A puddle in the cupboard means a leak. Steady dripping from the copper overflow pipe outside usually means the pressure relief valve — cheap fix, but it wastes power every hour it runs.</p>
      <p><strong>3. Warm but not hot.</strong> One failed element out of two, or a thermostat. Repairable in one visit on almost every NZ cylinder.</p>
      <p>Tell us what you found and we'll tell you on the phone what it likely is, what it costs, and whether it can safely wait until business hours — after-hours rates are real money and we'd rather you didn't pay them for a drip.</p>
